%T A208848 114,0,5539,0,0,605,0,119,29115
%N A208848 Number of non-Josephus subsets of n people.
%C A208848 A non-Josephus subset is a subset of people in the Josephus problem of a circle of n people for which no positive integer k exists which removes all those not in the subset first, by starting the count at person 1 and removing every k-th person clockwise.
%D A208848 R. L. Graham, D. E. Knuth and O. Patashnik, Exercise 1.26 in Concrete Mathematics, 2nd edition, Addison-Wesley, 1994, pages 26, 501.
%H A208848 James Dowdy and Michael E. Mays, <a href="https://www.researchgate.net/profile/M-Mays/publication/266756060">Josephus Permutations</a>, Journal of Combinatorial Mathematics and Combinatorial Computing 6 (1989), pages 125-130.
%H A208848 Martin Fuller, <a href="/A208848/a208848.cpp.txt">C++ & PARI programs</a>.
%H A208848 <a href="/index/J#Josephus">Index entries for sequences related to the Josephus Problem</a>.
%e A208848 For n = 9, the a(9) = 3 non-Josephus subsets are {1, 2, 5, 8, 9}, {2, 3, 4, 5, 8} and {2, 5, 6, 7, 8}.
%e A208848 There are 6 non-Josephus subsets for n = 28 that have less than n/2 elements, and no other examples for n <= 36: {1, 7..17, 28}, {1, 8..18, 28}, {1, 9..19, 28}, {1, 10..20, 28}, {1, 11..21, 28}, {1, 12..22, 28}. - _Martin Fuller_, Sep 28 2026
%o A208848 (C++ & PARI) /* See Fuller link. */
